<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz von Papen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz von Papen was a German Catholic conservative politician who was chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1932. He deposed the Social Democratic-led government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in July 1932 and in January 1933 helped persuade President Hindenburg to appoint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> as chancellor, serving as his vice-chancellor. He was later ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and was acquitted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen.jpg" alt="Franz von Papen" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1988-0113-500,_Franz_v._Papen_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Franz von Papen was born on 29 October 1879 in Werl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, into a wealthy noble Catholic family. His father was a Prussian cavalry officer. Papen entered a cadet school at the age of 11 and trained for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">German General Staff</a>, which he joined as a captain in March 1913. He became a military attaché in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in December 1913 and also observed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, where he was involved in selling arms to the government of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a>.</p><p>He later commanded a battalion on the Western Front, fighting on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">Somme</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vimy-ridge">Vimy Ridge</a>, and ended the war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> as a lieutenant colonel. After 1920 he entered politics as a monarchist on the national conservative wing of the Centre Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> appointed Papen chancellor on 1 June 1932, on the advice of General Kurt von Schleicher. His cabinet was nicknamed the cabinet of barons, and he had little support in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>. He governed by presidential decree, launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-coup-of-1932">Preußenschlag</a> against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> led by the Social Democratic Party, and dissolved the Reichstag twice. Hindenburg removed him in favour of Schleicher.</p><p>Papen met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> secretly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> on 4 January 1933 and on 22 January agreed to support him as chancellor with himself as vice-chancellor. Hindenburg appointed Hitler on 30 January. Papen believed Hitler could be contained, but Hitler and his allies quickly marginalised him. On 17 June 1934 he gave a speech at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-marburg">University of Marburg</a> calling for an end to SA terror. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/night-of-the-long-knives">Night of the Long Knives</a> his office was ransacked and associates including Herbert von Bose, Erich Klausener and Edgar Julius Jung were shot. Papen resigned, then served as ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from 1934 to 1938 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> from 1939 to 1944. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in 1938.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Papen was captured on 14 April 1945 and was one of the defendants at the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trial. The tribunal found no solid evidence that he had been involved in the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. It acquitted him, saying that his actions were political immoralities that were not punishable under the charge of conspiracy to commit crimes against peace. A West German denazification court later sentenced him to eight years of hard labour, but he was released on appeal in 1949. His memoirs of 1952 downplayed his involvement and portrayed him as a tragic patriot. He died on 2 May 1969. He is largely remembered for his role in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitlers-rise-to-power">Hitler's rise to power</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franz von Papen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 18 people died in the Altona Bloody Sunday on 17 July, Hindenburg used Article 48 to dismiss the Prussian ministers and appoint <b>Franz von Papen</b> Reich commissioner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The government of <b>Franz von Papen</b> deposed Braun’s cabinet in the coup of 20 July 1932 (the Preußenschlag), and under Nazi rule Prussia lost any real self-government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 30 January 1933 President Paul von Hindenburg appointed Hitler chancellor at the head of a coalition cabinet in which conservatives such as <b>Franz von Papen</b> expected to control him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From March 1930 President Paul von Hindenburg used Article 48 to back chancellors without a parliamentary majority: Heinrich Brüning, <b>Franz von Papen</b> and Kurt von Schleicher.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Werl, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Obersasbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.55,7.91&amp;year=1937" aria-label="Werl on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Franz von Papen"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5419 5695h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5419 5695h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.55,7.91&amp;year=1937">What was here in 1937? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
